kubeos-base (0.2.0-14) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Depend on kubeos-firewall, so every KubeOS node gets the INPUT default-deny firewall with
    the distribution itself instead of with a hand-run command. A node is an appliance on a
    network: with no firewall it answered on rpcbind 111, CoreDNS *:8080/*:8181 and etcd's client
    port on the LAN address (measured 2026-11-20), and none of that is anything an administrator
    asked for - it is what the components do by default.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:45:00 +0530

kubeos-base (0.2.0-13) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Make [Manager] settings actually apply on upgrade. system.conf.d is read by PID 1, so
    `daemon-reload` does not pick it up: after upgrading to 0.2.0-12 the watchdog stayed at
    RuntimeWatchdogUSec=0 on the live node until `systemctl daemon-reexec` was run by hand
    (measured 2026-11-20). postinst now re-execs systemd, which is the mechanism systemd uses
    for its own upgrades - so a node that upgrades gets the watchdog without a reboot.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:50:00 +0530

kubeos-base (0.2.0-12) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Enable the watchdog: /etc/systemd/system.conf.d/99-kubeos-watchdog.conf sets
    RuntimeWatchdogSec=30s and RebootWatchdogSec=10min. With the default of 0 nothing pets
    /dev/watchdog, so a hung kernel or a starved PID 1 leaves the machine dead until somebody
    power-cycles it - not what an appliance should do. 30s is a fast recovery and still longer
    than any normal stall; RebootWatchdogSec turns a shutdown that hangs half way into a hard
    reset instead of a machine that never comes back. Harmless where no watchdog device exists
    (systemd reports that it found none and continues).

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:30:00 +0530

kubeos-base (0.2.0-11) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Fix a silent no-op in 0.2.0-10: the security origin used ${distro_codename}, which comes
    from /etc/os-release where KubeOS's codename is "orhan" - so the pattern expanded to
    "orhan-security", matched no suite at all, and unattended-upgrade answered "No packages
    found that can be upgraded unattended". Automatic security updates would simply never have
    run, with nothing failing to show for it. The suite is now written out as trixie-security,
    and the dry run on a live node confirms the origin is matched.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:20:00 +0530

kubeos-base (0.2.0-10) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Apply Debian SECURITY updates automatically, and nothing else. A node is an appliance:
    nothing logs in to run apt, and a fresh install already showed 17 upgradable packages.
    Two files in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d: 21kubeos-auto-upgrades.conf (daily index + daily
    unattended run) and 52kubeos-unattended-upgrades.conf, which clears Debian's default
    origin list - it starts with "origin=Debian,codename=${distro_codename},label=Debian",
    i.e. the WHOLE trixie suite, so the stock policy would upgrade everything by itself - and
    allows only trixie-security. KubeOS and the Kubernetes stack are blacklisted by anchored
    regex (kubeos-*, kubelet, kubeadm, kubectl, kubernetes-cni, cri-tools, containerd.io,
    docker-*, helm), no automatic reboot, and nothing is ever removed automatically.
    The #clear form was verified against apt's own parser on a live node: the more obvious
    "#clear;" inside the list is rejected with "clear directive requires an option tree".

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:10:00 +0530

kubeos-base (0.2.0-9) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Package the enablement properly: the .wants link and the drop-in now use the canonical
    smartmontools.service (smartd is an alias symlink, which lintian rejected as
    "service-file-is-not-a-file"), the stale smartd.service link is gone from the payload, and
    the tag gets a documented override because its target belongs to the smartmontools package
    and is therefore invisible to lintian. Enabling through a shipped link is deliberate: a
    postinst `enable` would run in a live-build chroot with no systemd, so every fresh install
    would boot with disk-health monitoring off.
  * 0.2.0-8's drop-in is confirmed live on a node: `systemctl cat smartmontools` shows the
    vendor ExecStart replaced by /usr/sbin/smartd -n -c /etc/kubeos/smartd.conf, is-enabled
    answers "enabled", the config parses, and in a VM the unit is skipped (ConditionResult=no,
    Result=success) instead of failing.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:00:00 +0530

kubeos-base (0.2.0-8) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Fix the two things 0.2.0-7 got wrong, both caught on the live node before anyone else saw
    them: (1) the .wants link pointed at smartd.service, which is only an ALIAS symlink to
    smartmontools.service, so lintian failed the package with "service-file-is-not-a-file" and
    systemd was left with an ambiguous name - the link and the drop-in now use the canonical
    smartmontools.service; (2) the package shipped a drop-in but had no postinst, so nothing
    ran `systemctl daemon-reload` and `systemctl cat smartd` still showed the vendor ExecStart
    after the upgrade. postinst now reloads and starts the unit (a VM stays skipped by
    Debian's ConditionVirtualization=no, which is correct and not a failure).

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 19:50:00 +0530

kubeos-base (0.2.0-7) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Watch disk health: smartd is now enabled, with /etc/kubeos/smartd.conf
    (DEVICESCAN -H -f -t -n standby,q) and alerts that go to the journal instead of mail.
    smartmontools was installed on every node but its daemon never ran, so a disk could start
    failing its own SMART health check while the system reported nothing - the dashboard's
    Disks page shows usage, not health. Debian's own /etc/smartd.conf is left untouched (it
    belongs to smartmontools) and is replaced through a unit drop-in, because the default
    there mails root and a KubeOS node has no MTA, which would have lost the alert silently.
    Note: smartd.service carries ConditionVirtualization=no, so it is correctly inactive in
    a VM - this takes effect on physical hardware.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 19:40:00 +0530

kubeos-base (0.2.0-6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Cap crash dumps: /etc/systemd/coredump.conf.d/99-kubeos.conf sets MaxUse=200M,
    KeepFree=1G and ExternalSizeMax=512M. systemd's own defaults are 32 GB for a single dump
    and NO total limit, on a root filesystem of about 10 GB - so a crash-looping 300 MB
    process could fill / and take kubelet, etcd and the dashboard down, an outage caused by a
    diagnostic nobody was reading. The newest dumps (the ones `coredumpctl` is used for)
    survive; the oldest are dropped to stay inside the cap.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 19:25:00 +0530

kubeos-base (0.2.0-5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Ship /etc/apt/sources.list.d/debian.list - the base Debian suite was MISSING on every
    installed node. The installer's "deb cdrom:" line is disabled by preseed and nothing
    replaced it, and the ISO's other archive lists only cover security, updates and
    backports, so a package could be installed only if Debian had published a security
    update for it: on a freshly installed node `apt install htop` answered "has no
    installation candidate", as did strace, net-tools and systemd-coredump, while vim-nox
    and nfs-common installed purely because they had a DSA (measured 2026-11-20). It ships
    in this package rather than as an ISO archive list so that already-installed nodes are
    repaired by `apt update && apt upgrade`.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 19:20:00 +0530

